<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Nothing rounded up</div>
		<h1>What does not work</h1>
		<p class="lede">Read this before flashing. One phone is a daily driver; the
		other has no modem, which means it is not a phone.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Device</th><th>State</th><th>Not supported</th><th>Known issues</th></tr>
			<tr>
				<td>POCO X3 NFC<br><span class="mono" style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--accent)">xiaomi-surya</span></td>
				<td><span class="tag ok">daily driver</span></td>
				<td>Fingerprint<br>Stereo speakers</td>
				<td>Echo on calls<br>Shutdown freeze, cause unknown<br>Mic level unconfirmed</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>POCO X3 Pro<br><span class="mono" style="font-size:11.5px;color:var(--accent)">xiaomi-vayu</span></td>
				<td><span class="tag no">experimental</span></td>
				<td>Modem<br>IPA firmware not redistributable</td>
				<td>Modem dies 2&ndash;4&nbsp;s after the RF comes on</td>
			</tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>Fingerprint: no route left</h2></div>
		<p>The sensor is alive and its protocol is published. The bus is not
		reachable: the driver lives in the TrustZone and the SPI controller is never
		handed to Linux. Four routes measured, four dead ends &mdash; reading the
		stock driver, replaying the transfers, the pre-TrustZone window, the vendor
		blob. It needs a TrustZone exploit, which is a different project.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>02</span><h2>Stereo: kernel work, not configuration</h2></div>
		<p>Both speakers play, and they play the same thing. The I2S bus to the
		amplifiers carries one channel. Splitting it means changing
		<code>TERT_MI2S_RX</code> in the kernel; nothing in user space can produce a
		channel that is not being transmitted.</p>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>03</span><h2>Known issues, in order of annoyance</h2></div>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Echo on calls</th><td>The other end hears themselves. The canceller
			is wired and tunable at runtime; it needs patience and a second phone.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Shutdown freeze</th><td>Powering off works. The fix goes around the
			problem: the teardown still freezes and why is still unexplained.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>ADSP crash freezes the phone</th><td>An ADSP crash whose recovery
			deadlocks takes the root filesystem with it. The 2026-09-10 trigger is fixed
			and a watchdog now reboots instead of freezing; the deadlock is open.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>Microphone level</th><td>It records. Whether the gain is right has
			not been measured.</td></tr>
			<tr><th>GPS engine lock</th><td>Fixed here, and not this phone's fault:
			postmarketOS ships it locked on every Qualcomm device. Belongs
			upstream.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>

		<div class="h" style="margin-top:34px"><span>04</span><h2>POCO X3 Pro</h2></div>
		<p><b>The modem does not work, so it is not a phone.</b> It boots, the screen
		and touch work. The fault is pinned to one firmware assertion
		(<code>rflm_qlnk_ls_retry_cnt &lt; 2</code>): the modem asserts and takes
		itself down 2&ndash;4&nbsp;s after the RF comes on. Published so the research
		kernel and the patched <code>qcom-diag</code> are installable by anyone who
		wants to carry it further.</p>
